{"name":"Beaver","hex":"#926f5b","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Beaver","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname.","A number of places in the United States:\nA census-designated place in Yukon-Koyukuk Census Area, Alaska.\nA town in Carroll County, Arkansas.\nA minor city in Amaqua Township, Boone County, Iowa.\nA township and census-designated place therein, in Barton County, Kansas.\nAn unincorporated community in Floyd County, Kentucky.\nA township and unincorporated community therein, in Bay County, Michigan.\nAn unincorporated community in Baldwin Township, Delta County, Michigan.\nAn unincorporated community in Whitewater Township, Winona County, Minnesota.\nA village in Pike County, Ohio.\nA town, the county seat of Beaver County, Oklahoma.\nAn unincorporated community in Tillamook County, Oregon.\nA borough, the county seat of Beaver County, Pennsylvania.\nA city, the county seat of Beaver County, Utah.\nAn unincorporated community in Clallam County, Washington.\nA census-designated place in Raleigh County, West Virginia.\nA town in Clark County, Wisconsin.\nA town and unincorporated community therein, in Marinette County, Wisconsin.\nA town in Polk County, Wisconsin.\nA number of other townships in the United States, listed under Beaver Township."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native or resident of the American state of Oregon.","A member of the youngest wing of the Scout movement, composed of groups for children, traditionally boys, of approximately five to seven years of age."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Beaver"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beaver","blocks":[{"text":"Beavers are large semiaquatic rodents of the Northern Hemisphere. There are two extant species: the North American beaver and the Eurasian beaver. Beavers are the second-largest living rodents, after capybaras, weighing up to 50 kg (110 lb). They have stout bodies with large heads, long chisel-like incisors, brown or gray fur, hand-like front feet, webbed back feet, and tails that are flat and scaly. The two species differ in skull and tail shape and fur color. Beavers can be found in a number of freshwater habitats, such as rivers, streams, lakes and ponds. They are herbivorous, consuming tree bark, aquatic plants, grasses and sedges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/American_Beaver.jpg/330px-American_Beaver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaver","wikidata":"Q47542"},{"title":"Shades of brown","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of brown can be produced by combining red, yellow, and black pigments, or by a combination of orange and black—illustrated in the color box. The RGB color model, that generates all colors on computer and television screens, makes brown by combining red and green light at relatively low intensities, with green light being in a lesser proportion than red light. Brown color names are often imprecise, and some shades, such as beige, can refer to lighter rather than darker shades of yellow and red. Such colors are less saturated than colors perceived to be orange. Browns are usually described as light or dark, reddish, yellowish, or gray-brown. There are no standardized names for shades of brown; the same shade may have different names on different color lists, and sometimes one name can refer to several very different colors. The X11 color list of web colors has seventeen different shades of brown, but the complete list of browns is much longer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Color_icon_brown_v2.svg/330px-Color_icon_brown_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_brown","wikidata":"Q17079449"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Castor_range.png/500px-Castor_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Castor_range.png/1280px-Castor_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Castor_range.png/500px-Castor_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACastor_range.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Castor_canadensis1.jpg/500px-Castor_canadensis1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Castor_canadensis1.jpg/1280px-Castor_canadensis1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Castor_canadensis1.jpg/500px-Castor_canadensis1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACastor_canadensis1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Castor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg/500px-Castor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Castor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg/1280px-Castor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Castor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg/500px-Castor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACastor_fiber_eating_in_Eskilstuna%2C_Sweden.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Beaver_skeleton.jpg/500px-Beaver_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Beaver_skeleton.jpg/500px-Beaver_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mounted North American beaver skeleton.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeaver_skeleton.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Picture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png/500px-Picture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Picture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png/1280px-Picture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Picture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png/500px-Picture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The fore foot, hind foot, and tail of a beaver.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APicture_Natural_History_-_No_40_41_42_-_Beaver_feet_and_tail.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Beaver_tail_print_on_snow.jpg/500px-Beaver_tail_print_on_snow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Beaver_tail_print_on_snow.jpg/1280px-Beaver_tail_print_on_snow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Beaver_tail_print_on_snow.jpg/500px-Beaver_tail_print_on_snow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail